Chapter: CapGpuFamily
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 A bare [Gpu] row is a FAMILY grant and must reach the runtime.

 `Gpu` covers Gpu.Memory by the dotted sub-effect lattice, so this
 opening type-checks and gpu-mem-write is legal in its body. The
 question this test asks is whether the CAPABILITY survives the
 manifest: `manifest-cap-id` must know the name "Gpu", or the effect
 is silently dropped from the binary's capability section, the process
 never earns bit 18, and emit-gpu-mem-guard refuses the write.

 A denied write returns -1. A granted write returns 0 and the value
 reads back. So "write: 0" and "read: 4242" together mean the family
 grant travelled all the way from the row to the process capability
 word. That is BACKLOG 1.13.

Section: Body

  gpu-cmd : Integer = #BE000000

  opening : [Gpu, Console] Nothing =
    act
      w <- gpu-mem-write gpu-cmd 0 4242
      print-line-uni ("write: " & show w)
      r <- gpu-mem-read gpu-cmd 0
      print-line-uni ("read: " & show r)
    end

